window.__labLoaded({"id":"7.3","title":"Rank-Based Tests: When Averages Mislead","goal":"Show how rank-based tests (Mann-Whitney, Wilcoxon, Kruskal-Wallis) cope with an outlier that a t-test cannot, using the lesson's app-timing, pain and teaching data.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Two groups: the t-test versus ranks","text":"Task times in seconds for a new and an old app design. The old group has one 95-second outlier. Welch's t-test uses the size of every value, so the outlier hides the pattern. Mann-Whitney only uses the order of the values.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnew = [12, 15, 11, 14, 19, 13]\nold = [18, 22, 25, 17, 30, 95]\n\nt = stats.ttest_ind(new, old, equal_var=False)\nmw = stats.mannwhitneyu(new, old, alternative='two-sided', method='exact')\n\nprint(f'Welch t-test : t = {t.statistic:.2f}, p = {t.pvalue:.3f}')\nprint(f'Mann-Whitney : U = {mw.statistic:.0f}, p = {mw.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"Welch t-test : t = -1.67, p = 0.156\nMann-Whitney : U = 2, p = 0.0087","figs":[]},{"title":"How strong is the Mann-Whitney effect?","text":"U counts in how many of the 36 new-versus-old pairs the new design had the larger (slower) time. Here that is only 2, so almost every pair says the new design was faster. The rank-biserial correlation turns this into a size from 0 to 1.","code":"n1, n2 = len(new), len(old)\nu = mw.statistic\n\nr = 1 - 2 * u / (n1 * n2)\n\nprint(f'new time larger in {u:.0f} of {n1 * n2} pairs')\nprint(f'old time larger in {n1 * n2 - u:.0f} of {n1 * n2} pairs')\nprint(f'rank-biserial r = {r:.2f}')","out":"new time larger in 2 of 36 pairs\nold time larger in 34 of 36 pairs\nrank-biserial r = 0.89","figs":[]},{"title":"Paired data: Wilcoxon signed-rank","text":"Pain scores (0 to 10) for eight patients before and after treatment. The same people are measured twice, so we test the differences. The Wilcoxon statistic is the smaller of the two rank sums (positive and negative differences).","code":"before = [7, 8, 6, 9, 7, 6, 8, 7]\nafter = [3, 1, 4, 0, 4, 7, 2, 2]\n\nw = stats.wilcoxon(before, after)\npt = stats.ttest_rel(before, after)\n\nprint(f'Wilcoxon : W = {w.statistic:.0f}, p = {w.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'Paired t : t = {pt.statistic:.2f}, p = {pt.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"Wilcoxon : W = 1, p = 0.0156\nPaired t : t = 3.97, p = 0.0054","figs":[]},{"title":"Three groups: Kruskal-Wallis","text":"Three teaching methods with three students each. Kruskal-Wallis is the rank version of one-way ANOVA. With groups this small the p-value is an approximation, so treat it as a guide.","code":"a = [65, 72, 68]\nb = [80, 85, 78]\nc = [55, 60, 58]\n\nkw = stats.kruskal(a, b, c)\nf = stats.f_oneway(a, b, c)\n\nprint(f'Kruskal-Wallis: H = {kw.statistic:.2f}, p = {kw.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'ANOVA         : F = {f.statistic:.2f}, p = {f.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"Kruskal-Wallis: H = 7.20, p = 0.0273\nANOVA         : F = 38.78, p = 0.0004","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"new <- c(12, 15, 11, 14, 19, 13)\nold <- c(18, 22, 25, 17, 30, 95)\nt.test(new, old)\nwilcox.test(new, old)\nbefore <- c(7, 8, 6, 9, 7, 6, 8, 7)\nafter <- c(3, 1, 4, 0, 4, 7, 2, 2)\nwilcox.test(before, after, paired = TRUE)\nscore <- c(65, 72, 68, 80, 85, 78, 55, 60, 58)\nmethod <- factor(rep(c(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"), each = 3))\nkruskal.test(score ~ method)","expect":"Welch t = -1.6654, df = 5.0888, p-value = 0.1557; wilcox.test W = 2, p-value = 0.008658; paired V = 35, p-value = 0.01562; Kruskal-Wallis chi-squared = 7.2, df = 2, p-value = 0.02732","note":"For the paired test R reports V = 35 (the sum of the positive ranks) while Python reports W = 1 (the smaller of the two rank sums); both give the same p-value."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Nonparametric Tests > Legacy Dialogs > 2 Independent Samples (Mann-Whitney U) and 2 Related Samples (Wilcoxon)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / time group.\nBEGIN DATA\n12 1  15 1  11 1  14 1  19 1  13 1  18 2  22 2  25 2  17 2  30 2  95 2\nEND DATA.\nNPAR TESTS /M-W=time BY group(1,2).\nDATA LIST FREE / before after.\nBEGIN DATA\n7 3  8 1  6 4  9 0  7 4  6 7  8 2  7 2\nEND DATA.\nNPAR TESTS /WILCOXON=before WITH after (PAIRED).","note":"Group 1 is the new design. SPSS uses a normal approximation by default, so the Mann-Whitney U is 2 with Sig. about 0.01 (the exact value is 0.0087), and the Wilcoxon table shows rank sums 35 and 1 with Sig. about 0.02 (exact 0.0156). Kruskal-Wallis is /K-W=score BY method(1,3) on a dataset like the one in the Stata tab."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 12\ngenerate time = real(word(\"12 15 11 14 19 13 18 22 25 17 30 95\", _n))\ngenerate group = _n > 6\nranksum time, by(group)\nttest time, by(group) unequal\ngenerate before = real(word(\"7 8 6 9 7 6 8 7\", _n))\ngenerate after = real(word(\"3 1 4 0 4 7 2 2\", _n))\nsignrank before = after\ngenerate score = real(word(\"65 72 68 80 85 78 55 60 58\", _n))\ngenerate method = ceil(_n / 3) if _n <= 9\nkwallis score, by(method)","note":"ranksum is Mann-Whitney (it reports a z score and its p-value, about 0.010, and may also print the exact p-value 0.0087; group 0 is the new design). signrank is Wilcoxon (sum ranks 35 and 1) and kwallis is Kruskal-Wallis. The word() trick just enters the lesson's numbers quickly."},"try":["Change the 95 to 40 and then to 5000. Predict what happens to the Welch p-value and to the Mann-Whitney p-value, then check.","In the pain data, change patient 6's After score from 7 to 5. Does the Wilcoxon statistic get smaller or larger?"]});
